by nicolas-grekas at 2026-09-15T17:59:20Z

Thinking about the opt-in some more, I believe the Sylius case argues for a different shape than a firewall option.

A flag exists to protect existing code from a new call site. And it has the wrong granularity here: it is per firewall, but the safety property is per checker, and a firewall's checker is a `ChainUserChecker` of tagged services from packages the application does not control. Sylius's shop firewall chains one safe checker and two unsafe ones, so an application cannot opt in to the first without the other two. The flag puts the audit burden on the application author, who cannot see inside Sylius, instead of on the package author who wrote the checker.

An event has neither problem. It has no existing subscribers, so it needs no flag: writing the listener is the opt-in, and whoever writes it knows it runs on every request. Something like this, dispatched from `ContextListener::refreshUser()` on the firewall dispatcher it already holds, after the built-in comparison, with the verdict pre-seeded:

```php
final class CheckRefreshedUserEvent extends Event
{
    // token, originalUser, refreshedUser, bool $userChanged seeded by the built-in checks
    public function markUserAsChanged(?AuthenticationException $reason = null): void;
    public function setUserChanged(bool $changed): void;
}
```

#66052 becomes a five-line listener on `getRefreshedUser()->isEnabled()`. Running a user checker on refresh becomes a three-line listener with a try/catch, at whatever granularity the application or the package chooses, so the framework does not have to decide the pre/post question or the impersonation one: the listener sees the token. The comparison-shaped cases from the original issue get both users and the token, which `hasAdditionalChanges()` never had.

It is also the only shape with a path to a single way: a listener doing `setUserChanged(!$user->isEqualTo($refreshed))` reproduces `EquatableInterface` exactly, replace semantics included, so the interface becomes deprecable in a follow-up.

Most of this PR carries over: the deauthentication plumbing, the reason on `TokenDeauthenticatedEvent`, and the tests. What changes is the trigger, an event instead of an unconditional call into the checker chain.

What we lose is zero-code reuse of existing checkers. After the Sylius finding, that is the feature I would least want to keep.

